‎## 1. Observation — Free-Mode Constraint
‎
‎**Event:** Free-mode constraint interrupted active line of work.
‎
‎**Immediate temptation:** Treat restriction as problem to be solved by upgrading. Pay to open gate.
‎
‎**Fundamental question emerged:**
‎
‎> What exactly is the requirement? If task is commute, why invest in tractor?
‎
‎**This is [[Dispatch_018]] gate-evaluation signal in vivo:**
‎
‎- Limit encountered → Can I continue? (continuity gate)
‎- Not: How to pay past limit? But: What configuration allows work to continue without creating new dependency?
‎
‎**Distinction logged.**
‎
‎---
‎
‎## 2. The Experiment — Metaphor as Diagnostic Tool
‎
‎> The metaphor became diagnostic tool.
‎
‎| Element | Represents |
‎| :--- | :--- |
‎| Horse | Human intent |
‎| Cart | Work |
‎| Tractor | Excess capability purchased to overcome constraint |
‎| Gate | Platform-controlled access |
‎| Clock | Cost of lost time |
‎| Movement | Agency |
‎
‎**Critical observation:**
‎
‎> Arguing over whether horse should push or pull cart was irrelevant while both remained immobile. System did not need better argument. It needed movement.
‎
‎**Links to foundation:**
‎
‎- Dispatch 012: Command as invariant — system stuck, needs command that initiates movement, not better argument
‎- Rule #14.1: Non-decision is decision — immobility is response
‎- Dispatch 019: Time as denominator — clock cost, tide moves regardless
‎
‎**State machine:**
‎
‎Immobile (debating causality) → Movement (changed state) → New information → New choices → Agency restored
‎
‎Who moved first (push vs pull) does not solve equation. Movement changes state.
‎
‎---
‎
‎## 3. What Changed — Question Reframed
‎
‎**Initial question (surface):**
‎
‎> How do I get past the limit?
‎
‎Destination thinking. Gate-focused. Pay to open.
‎
‎**Better question (mechanism):**
‎
‎> What configuration allows work to continue without creating a new dependency?
‎
‎Invariant thinking. Agency-focused. Portability test.
‎
‎**Why distinction matters:**
‎
‎An upgrade may open immediate gate. It does not automatically establish that resulting workflow will remain consistent, predictable, or portable.
‎
‎**Per [[Dispatch_017]] preservation ledger:**
‎
‎- Cannot afford to lose: capability to continue work (movement, agency)
‎- Can replace: convenience of unlimited calls
‎- Should reduce: dependency on single gate that can re-close
‎- Cannot demonstrate value: paying for tractor to do commute
‎
‎Paying to escape constraint at precise moment of dependency is poor basis for decision-making.
‎
‎**Evaluation under pressure = information loss (Dispatch 019).**
‎
‎---
‎
‎## 4. Principle Candidates — Capability ≠ Suitability
‎
‎### Principle Candidate #15.1:
‎
‎> **Capability is not suitability. A more powerful tool is not necessarily a better tool.**
‎
‎Tractor is more capable than horse + cart. Not more suitable for commute.
‎
‎- More capability = more power, more features, more limits lifted
‎- Suitability = fit to requirement, portability, predictability, consistency
‎
‎**Tractor fails suitability test:**
‎
‎- Excess capability for task (commute doesn't need field-plowing power)
‎- Outside periphery (periphery = actual requirement)
‎- Creates new dependency (now dependent on tractor fuel, tractor gate, tractor pricing)
‎- Does not test portability (work still locked behind gate, just more expensive gate)
‎
‎### Principle Candidate #15.2:
‎
‎> **Never purchase continuity at the moment of dependency without first testing portability.**
‎
‎**Procedure:**
‎
‎1. Observe constraint (Free-mode limit)
‎2. Ask: What exactly is requirement? (commute vs field work?)
‎3. Test: Does upgrade establish consistency? Or just open gate temporarily?
‎4. Test: Can accumulated work travel? (Rule #08)
‎5. Test: Does this reduce or increase borrowed ground? (Rule #07)
‎6. Decide: Only after consistency established, not at peak dependency emotion
‎
‎**This is anti-impulse protocol.**
‎
‎- Impulse = pay to escape now, evaluate later
‎- Observation = observe now, decide with information later
‎
‎Correct response to uncertainty was observation — not impulse.
‎
